---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
const faqs = [
 { question: 'Which is better for a product release?', answer: 'Run the same audience, purchase-exit, inventory, and reply exercise in each. There is no measured winner established on this page.' },
 { question: 'Is Attentive always more expensive?', answer: 'No cost ordering is established. Compare its written proposal with a complete Postscript invoice model at the same message mix and service scope.' },
 { question: 'Must either be paired with Sequenzy?', answer: 'No. Evaluate native email availability or keep your existing provider after verifying event exchange and channel-specific exclusions.' },
 { question: 'Should both SMS senders run during migration?', answer: 'Plan a controlled handover with duplicate campaigns paused, reconciled permissions, and a rollback owner. Do not let overlapping queues contact the same audience unintentionally.' }
];
const path = '/compare/postscript-vs-attentive';
const title = 'Postscript vs Attentive: compare the workflow and the proposal';
const desc = 'Compare Postscript and Attentive using a shared trial brief, service scope, peak-month costs, and migration requirements.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} dateModified="2026-11-25" />
  <article class="container py-16 prose-shopify">
    <a href="/compare">All comparisons</a>
    <p class="badge mt-6">One brief for two proposals</p>
    <h1>Postscript vs Attentive for Shopify messaging</h1>
    <p>Evaluate these candidates against one unfinished job: acquiring permissioned subscribers, running a product release, or improving recovery. Neither store revenue nor an unsupported ranking determines the winner. This page provides a procurement and trial framework; no comparative performance test has been conducted here.</p>
    <h2>Documented differences to investigate</h2>
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Postscript</th><th>Attentive</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Pricing starting point</td><td>Published subscription tiers and message rates, with carrier fees and conditions</td><td>Modular proposal based on usage, subscribers, channels, and tools</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Email replacement</td><td>Shopify listing says email is rolling out in phases; establish account access</td><td>Pricing page offers email alongside text; establish the package scope</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Acquisition demonstration</td><td>Listing advertises Onsite Opt-in</td><td>Listing advertises two-tap signup units</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Support purchase</td><td>Request the tier and any additional service needed</td><td>Translate advertised strategic support into named deliverables</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Sources checked November 25, 2026: <a href="https://apps.shopify.com/postscript-sms-marketing">Postscript listing</a>, <a href="https://postscript.io/pricing">Postscript pricing</a>, <a href="https://apps.shopify.com/attentive">Attentive listing</a>, and <a href="https://www.attentive.com/pricing">Attentive pricing</a>. Features and support descriptions establish what to ask about, not observed superiority.</p>
    <h2>Use a release brief both vendors can reproduce</h2>
    <p>Choose one product release with a defined audience, a preference field, a recent-purchaser exclusion, and a landing page. Bring the same test contacts and timing requirements to both demos. Include an opted-out contact and one whose relevant preference is unknown. Ask each vendor to build the audience from the same inputs and explain every exclusion.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Capture a new signup and inspect the recorded permission and audience assignment.</li>
      <li>Schedule a test message, then purchase before its send time. Record whether the queue changes and how long the event takes to appear.</li>
      <li>Change inventory or variant availability. Inspect the destination and the team's cancellation procedure.</li>
      <li>Submit a support reply and an opt-out request. Follow each to the system and person responsible.</li>
      <li>Compare scheduled email with the SMS offer and identify who resolves a collision.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>These are proposed checks. Record failures, manual steps, and elapsed event times during your trial rather than marking a checklist passed from a feature page. For a scarce release, early access changes inventory available to later shoppers; do not call a difference in attributed sales a clean estimate of incremental lift.</p>
    <h2>Separate software from campaign labor</h2>
    <p>Ask both candidates for a responsibility table covering form design, segment creation, copy, approvals, scheduling, replies, and incident response. Establish the service hours and escalation route. If one proposal includes execution and the other includes advice, price the extra labor before declaring one platform cheaper.</p>
    <p>If your need is multi-store access or approval control, demonstrate it with your own team structure. If the need is a single release, demonstrate that release. Avoid buying a broad service package to solve a problem the existing sender can already handle with a corrected workflow.</p>
    <h2>Compare full invoices at the same volume</h2>
    <p>Postscript's public rate card makes a starting model possible, while Attentive requires a proposal for the selected scope. That difference is not evidence of which will cost less. Send both vendors a normal and peak-month forecast, separating automated traffic, broadcasts, SMS, MMS, destination markets, and optional products.</p>
    <p>Have each explain billable units, carrier charges, minimums, overages, and the effect of subscriber growth. Include onboarding, contract term, migration work, and any existing email fees you would retain. Use <a href="/guides/pricing">the cost worksheet</a> for the arithmetic; do not compare a headline per-message rate with a bundled invoice.</p>
    <h2>Decide email ownership before signing</h2>
    <p>Attentive markets email and SMS together; Postscript now markets email with phased availability. Ask for the email journeys, deliverability work, reporting, and account eligibility needed to replace your current provider. Neither is automatically an SMS-only layer, and neither requires adding Sequenzy by default.</p>
    <p>If you retain Klaviyo or another email service, establish which system owns each event and channel status. Postscript's listing names a Klaviyo integration, but that alone does not establish every field or suppression behavior. Evaluate any separate email tool on demonstrated data exchange, not the assumption that two products coordinate because both can import contacts.</p>
    <h2>Migration and final choice</h2>
    <p>Reconcile permissions and opt-outs before active audiences; preserve capture evidence and prevent historical events from restarting recovery flows. Plan how queued sends are paused and who restores the previous arrangement if an event mapping fails. Avoid letting two providers send the same campaign during a transition.</p>
    <p>Choose the proposal with demonstrated workflow fit, an affordable full invoice, and responsibilities your team can maintain. A failed purchase-exit or missing permission export is a concrete reason to delay the switch. A generic enterprise label is not. Read the individual <a href="/apps/postscript">Postscript</a> and <a href="/apps/attentive">Attentive</a> evaluations for their distinct trial emphasis.</p>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Postscript vs Attentive questions" />
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
